  "narrative": {
    "key_bets": [
      "End-plug electrostatic confinement at HTS-enabled mirror ratios of 10+ achieves Q > 5 in commercial conditions (never experimentally validated; Anvil demonstrator ~2028)",
      "Linear scaling thesis: each additional meter of center cell adds ~7 MW fusion power at roughly constant end-plug input power, enabling Q growth without proportional cost growth",
      "DCLC and Alfvén ion cyclotron mode stabilization via sloshing ions and vortex flows works at commercial densities and temperatures",
      "Lithium blanket (type undisclosed) achieves TBR > 1 with 14 MeV fusion neutrons in an axisymmetric cylindrical geometry",
      "Venetian blind direct energy converter survives continuous D-T fusion exhaust (alphas, neutrals, neutron activation) over a 30-year plant lifetime",
      "Recirculating power fraction stays low enough (~22-35%) for Qe > 1 net electricity at pilot and commercial scales",
      "HTS REBCO axisymmetric solenoid magnets can be radiation-shielded for 30-year operation in 14 MeV neutron environment without prohibitive coil-to-plasma standoff cost"
    ],
    "eliminated_costs": [
      "No disruption mitigation: linear mirror has no plasma current, eliminating disruption risk and simplifying vacuum vessel structural design vs. tokamaks",
      "Simpler magnet manufacturing: axisymmetric solenoid coils avoid 3D saddle (stellarator) or shaped D-coil (tokamak) winding complexity, lowering fabrication cost per unit REBCO tape",
      "No minimum economic size: avoids the tokamak's volume-driven minimum-plant-size constraint, potentially enabling smaller/cheaper plants",
      "Lower NBI port-area penalty: 50 m center cell allows NBI penetrations at small fractional port area",
      "Direct energy conversion recovers ~11% of D-T plant output from alpha kinetic energy that would otherwise require thermal conversion losses"
    ],
    "novel_costs": [
      "Venetian blind DEC electrode system with thin uncooled electrodes downstream of the fusion exhaust (no fusion-condition lifetime data)",
      "End divertor / heat-exhaust handling at the open-ended loss cones (heat flux on end structures and electrodes never analyzed for Hammir-class power)",
      "Multi-module center-cell solenoid string with demountable connections and per-meter coil cost not previously costed for any HTS axisymmetric design",
      "Continuous-wave gyrotron and NBI heating capex/opex (~$1M/MW gyrotrons, 40-100 MW input) sustained at steady state rather than pulsed",
      "Linear-machine remote handling and hot-cell access along the full length of an activated cylindrical plant for module replacement"
    ],
    "risks": [
      {
        "description": "End-plug confinement at Q > 5 has never been experimentally validated; Anvil demonstrator not built until ~2028. A factor-of-2 Q shortfall could push recirculating fraction above 50% and eliminate net electricity at modest plant sizes.",
        "severity": "high"
      },
      {
        "description": "Recirculating power fraction for commercial Hammir is undisclosed; parametric model estimates span 22.7-35%+ with input power assumptions ranging 40-100 MW (2.5x spread), directly threatening Qe > 1.",
        "severity": "high"
      },
      {
        "description": "Linear scaling thesis (~7 MW/m at constant input power) is physically plausible but cost-per-meter of center cell has never been estimated for an HTS axisymmetric design; advantage may be offset by structural/blanket cost growth.",
        "severity": "high"
      },
      {
        "description": "DCLC and Alfvén ion cyclotron instability stabilization solution still being developed computationally (ML-optimized); effectiveness at commercial densities and temperatures is unproven.",
        "severity": "high"
      },
      {
        "description": "Blanket type, TBR, and thermal cycle (steam vs. sCO2) are all proprietary/undisclosed; thermal efficiency could range 36-45% depending on choices, with TBR > 1 unverified at 14 MeV fluence.",
        "severity": "high"
      },
      {
        "description": "DEC electrode survivability under continuous D-T exhaust (neutron activation, alphas, neutrals) over 30-year lifetime is undemonstrated; failure or short lifetime hits availability and replacement cost.",
        "severity": "medium"
      },
      {
        "description": "REBCO supply chain concentration: $50M tape cost for sub-scale WHAM++ alone; commercial plant requires hundreds-thousands of km, with global capacity limited and CFS competing internally for SPARC/ARC demand.",
        "severity": "medium"
      },
      {
        "description": "Tritium supply (25-30 kg global inventory, ~$35,000/g) and Li-6 enrichment dependence on Russia/China suppliers are shared D-T constraints binding fleet-level deployment.",
        "severity": "medium"
      },
      {
        "description": "End divertor heat-exhaust loads and pumping/fueling at the open ends of a Hammir-class machine have never been analyzed in any Realta or recent mirror publication.",
        "severity": "medium"
      },
      {
        "description": "Linear-geometry remote handling for module replacement in an activated machine is a known practical challenge unaddressed in Realta's published materials, with potential capacity-factor and O&M implications.",
        "severity": "medium"
      },
      {
        "description": "Commercial plant size unpublished — Hammir pilot targets only > 50 MWe; the 500 MWe analysis assumption is an extrapolation, not a Realta target.",
        "severity": "medium"
      },
      {
        "description": "DEC contribution in D-T is thermodynamically capped at ~11% of plant output (20% alpha fraction × ~54% efficiency); excluding DEC raises LCOE only ~10-15%, so the differentiator is modest.",
        "severity": "low"
      }
    ]
  },
